1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:07,500
Two processes, one shared flag, and a section only one may enter.

2
00:00:07,500 --> 00:00:15,000
The obvious lock: test the flag, then set it. Two separate steps.

3
00:00:15,000 --> 00:00:22,500
Both read zero, both write one, both enter. The search finds it.

4
00:00:22,500 --> 00:00:30,000
Computed: 52 reachable states, exactly one with two inside.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:37,500
test-and-set returns the old value and stores one, as one step.

6
00:00:37,500 --> 00:00:45,000
The lock is one line: spin while the old value came back as one.

7
00:00:45,000 --> 00:00:52,500
The winner gets zero and enters. The others get one and spin.

8
00:00:52,500 --> 00:01:00,000
Computed: 135 states, no state with two inside, maximum one.

9
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:07,500
Written out, test-and-set is three statements, and that changes everything.

10
00:01:07,500 --> 00:01:15,000
Both load zero. Both store one. Both are told they won.

11
00:01:15,000 --> 00:01:22,500
The same search on the split version finds the same violation.

12
00:01:22,500 --> 00:01:30,000
So the load-bearing word in every definition here is indivisible.

13
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:37,500
swap exchanges two words indivisibly. Nothing else about it.

14
00:01:37,500 --> 00:01:45,000
A private key register of one, swapped with the lock word.

15
00:01:45,000 --> 00:01:52,500
The trace: lock zero to one, key one to zero, and this process is in.

16
00:01:52,500 --> 00:02:00,000
Computed: 425 states, no violation. Same lock, different instruction.

17
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:07,500
compare-and-swap takes three arguments: address, expected, new.

18
00:02:07,500 --> 00:02:15,000
As a lock: expect zero, ask for one, and spin while it fails.

19
00:02:15,000 --> 00:02:22,500
The same instruction, used instead to make an increment atomic.

20
00:02:22,500 --> 00:02:30,000
Computed: 2,000 increments, 2,000 in the counter, 1,000 retries.

21
00:02:30,000 --> 00:02:37,500
GATE 2012 gave a lock built on fetch-and-add, with a body that resets.

22
00:02:37,500 --> 00:02:45,000
The happy path: zero comes back, the word is one, and this process enters.

23
00:02:45,000 --> 00:02:52,500
The defect: a switch after the fetch, before the reset, and the word is stranded at one.

24
00:02:52,500 --> 00:03:00,000
Computed: a reachable state with the word at one and nobody inside, and no way in.

25
00:03:00,000 --> 00:03:07,500
The plain lock has no queue, so a process can lose every race.

26
00:03:07,500 --> 00:03:15,000
One flag per process, and the lock is handed over rather than raced for.

27
00:03:15,000 --> 00:03:22,500
On exit, scan forward for the next raised flag and clear it instead of the lock.

28
00:03:22,500 --> 00:03:30,000
Computed: 2,352 states, no violation, and the wait is at most n minus one.

29
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:37,500
Two counters: one dispenses tickets, one shows whose turn it is.

30
00:03:37,500 --> 00:03:45,000
The difference between the counters is the number of processes in the system.

31
00:03:45,000 --> 00:03:52,500
Computed: 1,201 states, no violation, and admission follows ticket order.

32
00:03:52,500 --> 00:04:00,000
The bound is n minus one admissions, with one caveat about wraparound.

33
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:07,500
A spinner burns the processor to ask the same question again.

34
00:04:07,500 --> 00:04:15,000
With n processes arriving together, the attempts are 0, 1, 2 and up to n minus one.

35
00:04:15,000 --> 00:04:22,500
Test-and-test-and-set: read cheaply, and use the instruction only when it may succeed.

36
00:04:22,500 --> 00:04:30,000
A blocking lock trades the spin for a queue and a switch.

37
00:04:30,000 --> 00:04:37,500
Every lock here gives mutual exclusion. Only two of them bound the wait.

38
00:04:37,500 --> 00:04:45,000
One indivisible instruction is the whole requirement, not the whole solution.

39
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:52,500
Read such a question in one order: instruction, then loop, then queue.

40
00:04:52,500 --> 00:05:00,000
So a lock is one indivisible step plus a queue, and nothing else.

